EPIC-260715-21g2pi_product-experience-and-security/STORY-260715-3tds7d_diagnostics-privacy-and-support/README.md:7:In scope: bounded runtime events and aggregate metrics, versioned diagnostic snapshots, approved and prohibited fields, redaction, retention and deletion behavior, diagnostics summary UI, support-export category preview, manifest, platform share/save handoff, pre-VPN privacy disclosure, settings disclosure, zero analytics and traffic telemetry verification, exit-host and system-exclusion explanations, accessibility, unit/security/UI tests, and physical acceptance. Out of scope: packet payload capture, DNS or destination logging, raw addresses, crash-report vendor integration, opt-out analytics for baseline, future telemetry, public privacy-policy hosting, legal licensing decisions, and release submission.
EPIC-260715-21g2pi_product-experience-and-security/STORY-260715-3tds7d_diagnostics-privacy-and-support/README.md:10:1. Default diagnostics are bounded and limited to approved timestamps, states, lane identifiers, aggregate counts, error domains/codes, memory observations, negotiated algorithms, relay identity, and redacted address-family facts. 2. Automated adversarial tests prove keys, passphrases, packet payloads, DNS names, destination hosts or IPs, full local addresses, shell input, and traffic samples cannot enter logs, snapshots, crash annotations, or support exports. 3. A user can inspect diagnostic categories, preview the exact export manifest, create an export only on explicit action, and delete retained local support data on iOS and macOS. 4. Before first VPN enablement the app explains processed data, the user-controlled SSH exit, exit-host visibility, Relux Works absence from the baseline data path, system-excluded traffic, retention/deletion, and zero traffic telemetry. 5. Extension-to-app diagnostic transfer is versioned, size-bounded, stale-generation safe, and remains useful after containing-app relaunch. 6. Unit, property/fuzz where appropriate, UI, and physical acceptance tests verify redaction, bounds, disclosure gating, export preview/output, deletion, accessibility, and no analytics or traffic-telemetry endpoints.
EPIC-260715-21g2pi_product-experience-and-security/STORY-260715-3tds7d_diagnostics-privacy-and-support/TASK-260715-3nzx7s_add-diagnostics-privacy-and-support-acceptance-tests/README.md:7:In scope: first-enable disclosure, dismiss/acknowledge/version update, settings revisit, diagnostics empty/current/historical/partial/error states, full/degraded parity, refresh, category preview, export success/cancel/failure, file inspection, delete, app relaunch/provider unavailable, iOS share/macOS save fixtures, identifiers, VoiceOver, keyboard, text scaling, screenshots, physical device checks, and network sentinel review. Out of scope: production traffic capture, public policy hosting, App Store submission, regional licensing, and support-ticket backend.
EPIC-260715-21g2pi_product-experience-and-security/STORY-260715-3tds7d_diagnostics-privacy-and-support/TASK-260715-3nzx7s_add-diagnostics-privacy-and-support-acceptance-tests/README.md:10:1. Page Object tests prove current disclosure acknowledgement gates first enable and every diagnostics/export/delete state behaves per contract on iOS and macOS. 2. Export test artifacts match the preview/manifest, contain only synthetic approved fields, pass prohibited-marker scanning, and leave no temporary copy after cancel/delete or completed handoff cleanup. 3. Accessibility checks cover identifiers, labels/values/hints, status announcements, focus, macOS keyboard, text scaling, contrast, reduced motion, and long approved copy. 4. Extracted screenshots and snapshot diffs are visually reviewed for layout, truncation, modal focus, progress, errors, orientation, and black screens. 5. A TASK-ID-scoped redacted result records simulator/macOS and named physical-device environments, commands, xcresults, screenshots, inspected export hashes/categories, network-sentinel outcome, and pass/fail per row.
EPIC-260715-21g2pi_product-experience-and-security/STORY-260715-3tds7d_diagnostics-privacy-and-support/TASK-260717-3ujeip_author-threat-model-and-user-facing-security-claims-doc/README.md:4:Complete the existing seed threat model and publish the bounded user-facing security claims contract that downstream disclosure, privacy, diagnostics, and policy work must consume. Reconcile claims against current architecture, ADRs, routing/DNS, relay, update, CI, and Keychain boundaries; distinguish implemented evidence, planned mitigations, residual risk, and explicit non-goals.
EPIC-260715-21g2pi_product-experience-and-security/STORY-260715-3tds7d_diagnostics-privacy-and-support/TASK-260717-3ujeip_author-threat-model-and-user-facing-security-claims-doc/README.md:7:In scope: audit and update .spec/threat-model.md; add a shippable claims source document under .spec; enumerate assets, data flows, adversaries, trust boundaries, mitigations, residual risks, abuse boundaries, non-goals, and change control; include a does-hide/does-not-hide matrix and claim-to-source crosswalk; verify consistency with .spec/architecture.md, .spec/security-privacy.md, .spec/routing-dns-lifecycle.md, .spec/relay-protocol.md, .spec/platform-distribution.md, and accepted ADRs; attach a task-scoped outcome and validation evidence. Out of scope: legal approval, App Store submission copy approval, new product behavior, runtime implementation, telemetry introduction, marketing claims not supported by evidence, external penetration testing, or secrets/user traffic in artifacts.
EPIC-260715-21g2pi_product-experience-and-security/STORY-260715-3tds7d_diagnostics-privacy-and-support/TASK-260717-3ujeip_author-threat-model-and-user-facing-security-claims-doc/README.md:10:1. .spec/threat-model.md is no longer a seed and covers all named assets, adversaries, data flows, trust boundaries, mitigations, residual risks, abuse boundaries, non-goals, and change-control triggers for macOS-first v1. 2. A separate shippable security-claims source gives plain-language approved and prohibited claims, including that SSH metadata remains visible and fingerprintable, the exit host sees destination metadata and plaintext, Relux Works is not in the baseline path, and fail-closed is platform-scoped rather than absolute. 3. Every positive claim is traced to an accepted ADR/spec and current implementation evidence or explicitly labeled planned/unverified; no planned mitigation is presented as shipped behavior. 4. The threat model and claims are internally consistent with the named source specs, contain no secrets or user traffic, and pass repository formatting, link/reference, board, and privacy checks. 5. A TASK-ID-scoped outcome records the claim crosswalk, changed files, validation commands, residual open evidence, and downstream consumers; a fresh independent Codex reviewer accepts the work before done.
EPIC-260715-21g2pi_product-experience-and-security/STORY-260715-n5dt84_onboarding-accessibility-and-migration/README.md:7:In scope: first-run and resumable onboarding, SSH host prerequisite guidance, privacy acknowledgement before VPN enablement, profile/trust/permission handoffs, empty and recoverable error states, settings/help entry points, VoiceOver and macOS accessibility, keyboard navigation, focus order, Dynamic Type and text scaling, contrast, reduced motion, localization-ready copy, pseudo-localization, shared accessibility identifiers, current SOCKS coexistence/replacement/retirement decision, approved defaults/history migration, iOS/macOS UI tests, and physical end-to-end acceptance. Out of scope: implementing a public SOCKS server, OpenSSH config import, ProxyJump, release notarization, TestFlight upload, App Store submission, regional licensing decisions, and unsupported locale translations.
EPIC-260715-21g2pi_product-experience-and-security/STORY-260715-n5dt84_onboarding-accessibility-and-migration/README.md:10:1. A first-time user on iOS and macOS can understand the self-hosted SSH model, complete or resume prerequisites, create a profile, trust a host, acknowledge privacy terms, grant VPN permission, connect, understand capability or failure, obtain help, and disconnect. 2. Privacy disclosure appears before the first enable action and onboarding does not claim anonymity, a Relux exit network, or capabilities not present in the selected mode. 3. All M4 surfaces have stable shared accessibility identifiers, meaningful labels/hints/values, logical focus, keyboard operation on macOS, VoiceOver operation, supported text scaling, sufficient contrast, and reduced-motion behavior. 4. User-facing strings are externalized, pseudo-localization and text expansion pass, and the approved launch-locale policy and fallback are documented. 5. A reviewed decision selects legacy SOCKS coexistence, replacement, or retirement and defines handling of host/account/port defaults, existing release history, bundle/storage identity, rollback, and user messaging before migration code runs. 6. Automated UI journeys plus named physical Apple-silicon Mac acceptance, with the iPhone journey deferred with iOS under ADR-024, cover first run, relaunch/resume, permission denial, empty/error recovery, migration path, full/degraded/failure states, diagnostics, accessibility, and screenshot review.
