EPIC-260715-21g2pi_product-experience-and-security/STORY-260715-tx1tbz_profile-and-key-management/TASK-260715-1idq8c_establish-shared-apple-ui-test-infrastructure/README.md:7:In scope: a shared test constants target, BEM-like identifiers, typed launch arguments/environment, PageElement and ComponentElement protocols, PageManager composition, deterministic profile/trust/session/diagnostic fixtures, XCUITest result bundles, step screenshots, extraction into task-scoped temporary output, Swift Testing snapshot suites where appropriate, failed-snapshot diff artifacts, iOS Simulator and native macOS destinations, and documentation of physical-device-only rows. Out of scope: product screens, production credentials, wall-clock sleeps, Allure unless already selected by the project, running iOS UI tests on Designed-for-iPad Mac, and business-specific test assertions.
EPIC-260715-21g2pi_product-experience-and-security/STORY-260715-tx1tbz_profile-and-key-management/TASK-260715-1idq8c_establish-shared-apple-ui-test-infrastructure/README.md:10:1. Shared identifiers and typed test arguments compile in both containing apps and both UI-test targets with one source of truth and no raw string duplication. 2. Page Objects expose descriptive waits/actions and use condition-based synchronization rather than fixed sleeps. 3. Deterministic fixtures can launch each required profile, trust, capability, failure, diagnostic, onboarding, migration, and privacy state without embedding real secrets or bypassing production safety decisions. 4. A smoke run on an available iOS Simulator and native macOS test destination produces xcresult bundles, step-named screenshots, extracted images, and snapshot diff artifacts on failure. 5. The task documents mandatory visual screenshot inspection for orientation, layout, content, and black-screen failures plus the separate named physical-device evidence contract.
EPIC-260715-21g2pi_product-experience-and-security/STORY-260715-tx1tbz_profile-and-key-management/TASK-260715-1kfqgp_add-profile-and-key-cross-platform-ui-tests/README.md:10:1. macOS Page Objects cover one successful and every specified recovery path, with the iOS rows deferred with iOS under ADR-024 and re-armed unchanged when iOS resumes, with conditional waits and no fixed sleeps. 2. Tests assert field errors, action enablement, selected/active state, confirmations, retries, relaunch persistence, and zero secret text in labels, screenshots, logs, or result attachments. 3. Step screenshots are extracted and visually reviewed for orientation, layout, content, focus, text scaling, and black-screen failures; snapshot diffs are retained on failure. 4. Accessibility assertions cover identifiers, roles, labels, values, hints, focus order, keyboard commands on macOS, and representative Dynamic Type/text scaling. 5. The task records simulator/macOS destinations, versions, commands, xcresult paths, screenshot review evidence, and any physical-device rows deferred to M4 acceptance.
EPIC-260715-21g2pi_product-experience-and-security/STORY-260715-tx1tbz_profile-and-key-management/TASK-260715-6ig5xj_implement-private-key-import-and-validation/README.md:7:In scope: iOS document picker and macOS open-panel adapters behind a shared importer, security-scoped URL lifetime, size limits, approved OpenSSH/PEM or engine-specific formats, encrypted-key detection, passphrase validation without retention unless chosen, algorithm and public fingerprint extraction, duplicate identification, metadata naming, cancellation, malformed and permission errors, temporary cleanup, and deterministic fixtures. Out of scope: OpenSSH config import, public-key-only authentication entries, filesystem permission changes outside user-selected files, silent export, password auth, and UI layout beyond adapter contracts.
EPIC-260715-21g2pi_product-experience-and-security/STORY-260715-tx1tbz_profile-and-key-management/TASK-260721-2raag7_add-explicit-exit-resolver-profile-experience/README.md:7:In scope: shared presentation state and actions; editing, adding, removing, and reordering a non-empty endpoint list within the accepted endpoint-count policy from TASK-260721-3miqh4; IPv4 and IPv6 literal plus port entry; port 53 presentation default; field-level validation; requiresResolverConfiguration remediation; repository publication and revision conflicts; active-profile safeguards; iOS and macOS SwiftUI surfaces; accessibility identifiers; snapshots; Page Object UI tests. Out of scope: changing TASK-260715-1tnjlu policy, selecting or prefilling a public resolver, hard-coding an unevidenced count ceiling, hostname endpoints, DoH, exit-host discovery, DNS transport implementation, final marketing copy, and physical leak validation.
EPIC-260715-21g2pi_product-experience-and-security/STORY-260715-tx1tbz_profile-and-key-management/TASK-260721-2raag7_add-explicit-exit-resolver-profile-experience/README.md:10:1. The shared editor and both Apple surfaces present a required ordered list of numeric resolver endpoints with address and port controls, enforce the accepted DNSRuntimePolicyV1 endpoint-count ceiling, default an omitted port to 53, and never prefill a product, physical, public-vendor, loopback, or discovered resolver address. 2. Validation and normalized save values exactly match TASK-260721-33o8fc and TASK-260721-3miqh4, including deliberate loopback, private, and ULA support and field-specific errors for hostnames, zones, invalid families, duplicates, empty or policy-oversized lists, and ports. 3. A legacy profile without dnsResolver remains visible with requiresResolverConfiguration remediation, cannot be selected for a new connection until valid input is saved, and is never silently upgraded from system DNS. 4. Add, remove, reorder, save, discard, relaunch, concurrent revision conflict, selected-profile change, active-profile edit, and endpoint-count boundary paths produce deterministic repository generations and accessible recovery on iOS and macOS. 5. Swift tests, snapshots, and iOS and macOS Page Object journeys cover valid IPv4-only, IPv6-only, dual ordered, loopback, invalid, policy-count boundary, migration, and conflict states with stable identifiers, keyboard and VoiceOver behavior, screenshot inspection, and no DNS network dependency.
EPIC-260715-2lz67t_udp-relay-and-degraded-mode/STORY-260715-18ncz1_relay-protocol-conformance/README.md:7:In scope: relay implementation and binding strategy; protocol v1 constants; client and server hello; feature and maximum-frame negotiation; incremental envelope parsing; IPv4, IPv6, and domain HEV UDP payloads; client-allocated association IDs; PING, PONG, close, and bounded error messages; reserved flags and unknown types; allocation ceilings; generated vectors; fragmentation, coalescing, hostile-input, fuzz, and compatibility tests. Out of scope: opening UDP sockets, building release assets, remote upload or installation, tunnel capability state, physical-device traffic validation, and protocol v2 behavior.
EPIC-260715-2lz67t_udp-relay-and-degraded-mode/STORY-260715-18ncz1_relay-protocol-conformance/TASK-260715-297gq6_add-cross-language-protocol-conformance-and-fuzz-tests/README.md:7:In scope: both corpus consumers; every incremental cut; multiple-frame permutations; mutated magic, version, status, lengths, flags, type, associationID, inner payload, address, and close sequences; deterministic fuzz seeds; coverage corpus; allocation and iteration ceilings; cancellation; sanitizer or runtime diagnostics supported by each toolchain; CI commands. Out of scope: network fuzzing against public hosts, relay socket behavior, asset installation, physical-device performance, retaining real packets, and weakening expected failures per implementation.
EPIC-260715-2lz67t_udp-relay-and-degraded-mode/STORY-260715-1nsw9p_udp-forwarding-and-associations/README.md:7:In scope: client association allocation and non-reuse; HEV frame translation; SSH exec-channel frame pump; relay association registry and rootless UDP sockets; IPv4, IPv6, and domain destinations; response source-address preservation; errors, idle expiry, and explicit close; maximum datagram, frame, association, queued-byte, and timer limits; DNS priority without protocol privilege; full-mode DNS over relay with existing TCP fallback semantics; counters; conformance, pressure, fuzz, soak, harness, and physical IPv4 or IPv6 validation. Out of scope: direct TCP implementation, remote asset installation, physical-interface UDP fallback, fake DNS, M3 lane scheduling or path migration, final QUIC product policy, and public relay listeners.
EPIC-260715-2lz67t_udp-relay-and-degraded-mode/STORY-260715-1nsw9p_udp-forwarding-and-associations/README.md:10:1. Representative IPv4 and IPv6 UDP datagrams traverse HEV, the framed SSH relay session, and user-host sockets with byte integrity and responses carry the source endpoint observed by the relay. 2. Domain destinations and tunnel-owned DNS work through the relay, including DNS priority and existing TCP fallback semantics, without any physical resolver or UDP path. 3. Association creation, close, expiry, error, and permitted ID reuse are deterministic on client and relay and session loss invalidates all association state. 4. Negotiated caps bound frames, datagrams, associations, sockets, queued bytes, timers, and work per event-loop turn; saturation drops or rejects with reason-specific counters and never grows an unbounded retry buffer. 5. Automated and physical evidence covers nominal traffic, hostile frames and addresses, oversize, queue pressure, expiry, process loss, repeated cleanup, IPv4, IPv6, DNS, memory, and privacy-safe diagnostics.
EPIC-260715-2lz67t_udp-relay-and-degraded-mode/STORY-260715-1nsw9p_udp-forwarding-and-associations/TASK-260715-1ut6ot_add-udp-association-and-datagram-conformance-tests/README.md:7:In scope: fake HEV streams; fake and loopback SSH channels; controlled UDP echo and DNS fixtures; client and relay registries; all address types; multiple destinations per association where supported; request and response byte integrity; source endpoints; ID lifecycle; PING or PONG coexistence; errors and closes; fake clock expiry; configured boundary limits; cancellation; repeated start and stop. Out of scope: arbitrary malformed-byte fuzzing owned separately, public Internet endpoints, physical Apple devices, long soak, bootstrap installation, path transitions, and performance claims.
EPIC-260715-2lz67t_udp-relay-and-degraded-mode/STORY-260715-1nsw9p_udp-forwarding-and-associations/TASK-260715-1ut6ot_add-udp-association-and-datagram-conformance-tests/README.md:10:1. Table tests cover every legal association state and protocol message combination for IPv4, IPv6, domain, empty or maximum payload, source response, error, local close, remote close, expiry, and session loss. 2. Composed tests send concurrent bidirectional datagrams through HEV adapter, client pump, protocol peers, relay sockets, and controlled destinations with byte hashes and association identities intact. 3. DNS fixtures prove priority UDP relay behavior and approved TCP fallback on truncation or relay failure without a physical resolver sentinel hit. 4. Boundary runs admit up to each configured limit, reject the next unit deterministically, preserve control progress, and reconcile frames, associations, sockets, timers, queue bytes, drops, and errors. 5. Repeated fake-clock scenarios return all tasks, channels, associations, descriptors, buffers, timers, resolver work, and counters to documented baseline without sleeps or timing flakes.
EPIC-260715-2lz67t_udp-relay-and-degraded-mode/STORY-260715-1nsw9p_udp-forwarding-and-associations/TASK-260715-24e2o1_document-udp-limits-metrics-and-operations/README.md:10:1. Diagrams and tables trace one datagram and response through every component and state who owns frame, association, buffer, socket, timer, and cleanup at each boundary. 2. Exact configured and negotiated limits, hard ceilings, admission order, priority classes, saturation drops, error codes, idle expiry, and ID reuse conditions match implemented tests and diagnostics. 3. DNS documentation covers approved resolver, UDP relay priority, TC and safe TCP fallback, cache interaction, failure behavior, and zero physical resolver path. 4. Reproduction sections name local, conformance, fuzz, pressure, soak, and Apple-silicon Mac commands, marking iPhone commands as deferred under ADR-024, with expected counters, captures, memory fields, and redaction steps. 5. Troubleshooting covers address, oversize, exhaustion, queue pressure, resolution, socket, relay process, protocol, and cleanup failures and links capability, M3, and release handoffs by board ID.
EPIC-260715-2lz67t_udp-relay-and-degraded-mode/STORY-260715-1nsw9p_udp-forwarding-and-associations/TASK-260715-28jdml_integrate-full-mode-dns-over-relay/README.md:7:In scope: consume the approved resolver identity, M1 DNS listener, cache, and TCP manager; relay UDP to the generation-global active IPv4 or IPv6 endpoint; DNS-priority association policy; UDP response correlation; EDNS and negotiated datagram cap; client transport semantics; exactly enumerated same-endpoint TCP retry triggers; handoff to M1 TCP connection and promotion ownership; shared logical deadline, byte, time, and retry accounting from DNSRuntimePolicyV1; cancellation; cache and health events; no physical resolver; metrics without query names. Out of scope: choosing a new resolver, parsing or caching anew, DoH, recursive resolution, fake DNS, general UDP admission, physical-interface sockets, response-based resolver shopping, and logging questions or answers.
EPIC-260715-2lz67t_udp-relay-and-degraded-mode/STORY-260715-1nsw9p_udp-forwarding-and-associations/TASK-260715-28jdml_integrate-full-mode-dns-over-relay/README.md:10:1. Client TCP DNS enters the existing listener and goes directly to the M1 SSH TCP manager; eligible client UDP DNS makes at most one relay UDP transmission to the active configured endpoint, and no DNS path opens a physical socket or invokes a device resolver. 2. A complete valid non-truncated UDP response, including any RCODE, is authoritative and ends the logical query without endpoint promotion; transaction ownership preserves IDs, EDNS, DNSSEC transparency, cache semantics, cancellation, and exactly one visible client result. 3. M2 owns a same-endpoint protocol retry from UDP to TCP only after TC=1, a local or negotiated relay-datagram size failure before a valid response, UDP response timeout, or a typed relay association or session transport failure; malformed or mismatched UDP data is rejected and does not trigger resolver shopping. 4. The same logical deadline and accounting allow at most one UDP attempt plus at most one TCP attempt per configured endpoint: M1 owns TCP connection reuse and may promote serially to each later not-yet-attempted endpoint only after TCP transport or protocol failure, never after a valid DNS response. 5. Controlled fixtures cover IPv4 and IPv6 endpoints, UDP success, TC, local oversize, timeout, association error, relay loss, client TCP, same-endpoint TCP recovery, later-endpoint TCP promotion, malformed UDP rejection, cache hit and miss, cancellation, priority and byte pressure, query-name-free metrics, one visible answer, and zero physical sentinel observations.
EPIC-260715-2lz67t_udp-relay-and-degraded-mode/STORY-260715-1nsw9p_udp-forwarding-and-associations/TASK-260715-cqm7m5_add-udp-hostile-pressure-fuzz-and-soak-tests/README.md:7:In scope: mutation corpus for protocol and HEV frames; arbitrary chunking; invalid association IDs and reuse; domain and sockaddr edge cases; oversize and truncation; many associations; per-association and global queue pressure; stalled SSH and UDP receivers; DNS priority abuse; resolver timeout; socket errors; relay exit; cancellation; allocation ceilings; deterministic seeds; hours-scale controlled soak; log redaction scans. Out of scope: public-target fuzzing, denial of service against shared infrastructure, physical path transitions, product analytics, real traffic capture, optimizing after failed tests, and waiving hard caps.
EPIC-260715-2lz67t_udp-relay-and-degraded-mode/STORY-260715-2ungml_capability-and-degraded-mode/README.md:7:In scope: capability bit and reason taxonomy; full, degraded, failed, and reasserting-compatible state contract; relay build and limit evidence; versioned provider snapshots; bootstrap, handshake, process, and lane failure mapping; association invalidation; deterministic degraded safe-DNS policy using the existing SSH DNS-over-TCP path or an explicitly approved tunneled DoH alternative; TCP continuity; explicit UDP and QUIC-family rejection policy input; fail-safe DNS behavior; bounded reprobe and full-mode restoration; diagnostics, user and operator documentation; state, fault, leak, harness, and physical transition tests. Out of scope: implementing TCP direct forwarding, M3 multi-lane scheduling, general path-change reconnect, includeAllNetworks fail-closed mode, captive or NAT64 transitions, final UI visuals, analytics, and silent physical fallback.
EPIC-260715-2lz67t_udp-relay-and-degraded-mode/STORY-260715-2ungml_capability-and-degraded-mode/README.md:10:1. A versioned capability model distinguishes usable full mode, usable degraded TCP plus safe DNS, mandatory failure, and reasserting-compatible transitions with stable privacy-safe reason codes. 2. Unsupported platform, absent asset, bootstrap failure, checksum or version mismatch, relay exit, framing failure, and relay-lane loss invalidate UDP associations and enter degraded mode only when TCP and the selected safe-DNS path are actually usable. 3. In degraded mode TCP remains usable, DNS uses only the approved tunneled fallback, ordinary UDP is rejected explicitly, and controlled access-side monitoring observes zero ordinary DNS or application UDP fallback to the physical interface. 4. Full mode is advertised again only after a fresh validated relay handshake and limits exchange; stale callbacks or sessions cannot resurrect capability. 5. Automated and physical transition evidence covers startup degraded, full-to-degraded, degraded-to-full, safe-DNS failure, stop, repeated reprobe, bounded memory, truthful snapshots, redacted diagnostics, and user-facing limitations.
EPIC-260715-2lz67t_udp-relay-and-degraded-mode/STORY-260715-2ungml_capability-and-degraded-mode/TASK-260715-1vg1mb_add-capability-state-and-fault-injection-tests/README.md:7:In scope: table-driven state transitions; fake base TCP, safe DNS, bootstrap, relay, association, health, clock, jitter, memory, and snapshot dependencies; every reason code; startup full and degraded; runtime loss; simultaneous failures; safe-DNS mandatory failure; restore; stop; duplicate and late events; provider-message versions; counters and cleanup. Out of scope: real sockets, remote hosts, physical routing captures, M3 path reconnect, final UI snapshot tests, protocol byte fuzzing, and performance tuning.
EPIC-260715-2lz67t_udp-relay-and-degraded-mode/STORY-260715-2ungml_capability-and-degraded-mode/TASK-260715-2y78ah_add-degraded-routing-dns-leak-integration-tests/README.md:1:# Add degraded routing, DNS leak, and recovery integration tests
EPIC-260715-2lz67t_udp-relay-and-degraded-mode/STORY-260715-2ungml_capability-and-degraded-mode/TASK-260715-2y78ah_add-degraded-routing-dns-leak-integration-tests/README.md:4:Exercise the composed M2 runtime in controlled harness and macOS development contexts across startup full or degraded, relay failures, UDP rejection, TCP continuity, safe DNS, mandatory DNS loss, reprobe restoration, and physical-path sentinels.
EPIC-260715-2lz67t_udp-relay-and-degraded-mode/STORY-260715-2ungml_capability-and-degraded-mode/TASK-260715-2y78ah_add-degraded-routing-dns-leak-integration-tests/README.md:7:In scope: composed M1 packet, TCP, DNS, SSH, and provider seams; M2 bootstrap and relay fixtures; controlled TCP, UDP, and DNS endpoints; access-side physical DNS and UDP sentinels; full startup; unsupported asset; checksum or version failure; process and lane failure; general UDP rejection; safe DNS-over-TCP or approved DoH; TCP continuity; DNS loss; recovery; stop; counters; repeated runs; redaction. Out of scope: physical iPhone acceptance, path switching, NAT64 transition, captive portal, includeAllNetworks, lane pool scheduling, final QUIC policy, and public resolver benchmarking.
EPIC-260715-2lz67t_udp-relay-and-degraded-mode/STORY-260715-2ungml_capability-and-degraded-mode/TASK-260715-2y78ah_add-degraded-routing-dns-leak-integration-tests/README.md:10:1. Full startup carries TCP, general UDP, and DNS through the intended SSH paths, while startup relay failure publishes degraded only after TCP and safe DNS are usable. 2. Every full-to-degraded trigger stops and invalidates UDP before the snapshot, preserves controlled TCP according to M1 policy, and keeps client UDP and TCP DNS on the approved tunneled fallback. 3. Physical DNS and UDP sentinels observe zero ordinary fallback during connected, transition, degraded, failed, reprobe, and stop fault rows; safe-DNS loss produces mandatory failure. 4. Reprobe restores full only after a fresh validated relay generation and subsequent UDP and DNS traffic uses new associations with no late old-generation delivery. 5. Repeated scenarios reconcile system session, capability snapshots, TCP flows, DNS transactions, relay generation, associations, queues, channels, timers, routes, drops, and memory and return resources to baseline.
EPIC-260715-2lz67t_udp-relay-and-degraded-mode/STORY-260715-2ungml_capability-and-degraded-mode/TASK-260715-2zmw58_record-degraded-safe-dns-fallback-policy/README.md:4:Translate the approved M1 exit-resolver decision into an exact M2 degraded-mode upstream selection and health contract, using the existing SSH DNS-over-TCP implementation by default or a separately approved tunneled DoH path without physical fallback.
EPIC-260715-2lz67t_udp-relay-and-degraded-mode/STORY-260715-2ungml_capability-and-degraded-mode/TASK-260715-2zmw58_record-degraded-safe-dns-fallback-policy/README.md:7:In scope: consume TASK-260715-1tnjlu and resulting resolver schema; DNS-over-TCP direct-tcpip baseline; conditions for configured tunneled DoH if the approved policy includes it; resolver endpoint and identity; address-family order; transport priority; timeout and retry; connection pooling input; TC behavior; cache generation; health readiness; fail-safe outcome; profile migration; test fixtures; privacy and UI implications. Out of scope: selecting an unapproved public resolver, physical DNS, remote shell resolver discovery without a separate security design, fake DNS, implementing transports, final profile UI, and silently changing the M1 resolver contract.
EPIC-260715-2lz67t_udp-relay-and-degraded-mode/STORY-260715-2ungml_capability-and-degraded-mode/TASK-260715-2zmw58_record-degraded-safe-dns-fallback-policy/README.md:10:1. A TASK-ID-scoped decision references the approved M1 resolver policy and states exact degraded resolver fields, defaults, transport, endpoint or URL identity, ports, address-family behavior, timeouts, retries, and cache invalidation. 2. DNS-over-TCP through authenticated SSH is the deterministic baseline unless the approved policy explicitly selects a certificate-validated tunneled DoH alternative and its trust inputs. 3. Startup and runtime readiness rules prove degraded mode is not usable until one safe upstream is ready and that upstream failure cannot trigger physical resolver, exit-host guesswork, or unapproved vendor fallback. 4. The decision covers client UDP and TCP DNS, TC or oversized responses, DNSSEC transparency, cancellation, profile change, relay restoration, diagnostics, and zero query-name logging. 5. Downstream integration, state, leak-test, documentation, profile, and UI tasks are listed by concrete ID; any unresolved accountable resolver choice remains on the existing M1 decision instead of being guessed here.
EPIC-260715-2lz67t_udp-relay-and-degraded-mode/STORY-260715-2ungml_capability-and-degraded-mode/TASK-260715-3260rm_integrate-selected-degraded-safe-dns-transport/README.md:7:In scope: consume M1 DNS listener, transaction, cache, resolver, and SSH upstream components; selected transport construction; authenticated SSH dependency; readiness probe; UDP and TCP client queries; TC and length semantics; bounded connections, requests, bytes, timeouts, and retries; cache invalidation on resolver or generation change; health loss; cancellation; typed failure; metrics without query data. Out of scope: relay UDP, physical resolver fallback, new resolver selection, recursive server implementation, fake DNS, general HTTP client outside approved DoH scope, path reconnect, and final UI.
EPIC-260715-2lz67t_udp-relay-and-degraded-mode/STORY-260715-2ungml_capability-and-degraded-mode/TASK-260715-3260rm_integrate-selected-degraded-safe-dns-transport/README.md:10:1. Degraded DNS accepts the existing virtual-network UDP and TCP clients and sends every upstream request only through the selected authenticated tunneled transport to the approved resolver. 2. Readiness is not published until transport construction and a deterministic safe health condition succeed, and runtime loss emits a typed mandatory failure before any physical fallback. 3. TCP framing or approved DoH request and certificate behavior, correlation, maximum message size, TC, timeout, cancellation, cache generation, and retry rules match the recorded policy and M1 transaction contract. 4. Connections, channels, requests, response buffers, cache entries, timers, and retries remain within fixed ceilings and return to baseline on mode change, profile change, SSH loss, and stop. 5. Controlled resolver and fake-channel tests cover both client transports, IPv4 and IPv6 resolver endpoints where approved, success, malformed response, timeout, certificate failure if applicable, cancellation, repeated cleanup, and zero physical sentinel observations.
EPIC-260715-2lz67t_udp-relay-and-degraded-mode/STORY-260715-2ungml_capability-and-degraded-mode/TASK-260715-ak0s72_implement-relay-failure-to-degraded-transition-controller/README.md:7:In scope: unsupported platform or asset; bootstrap, upload, verification, install, launch, handshake, feature, and limit failure; relay framing, health, process, or lane loss; association admission stop; client and relay state invalidation; queue and timer cleanup; safe-DNS readiness check; TCP preservation; snapshot transition; terminal failure when base path is unsafe; stop cancellation; generation safety; diagnostics. Out of scope: repairing SSH path loss, selecting a new network path, opening lane pools, M3 reasserting implementation, implementing DNS transport, QUIC policy detail, and physical route mutation beyond existing M1 safety.
EPIC-260715-2lz67t_udp-relay-and-degraded-mode/STORY-260715-2ungml_capability-and-degraded-mode/TASK-260715-ak0s72_implement-relay-failure-to-degraded-transition-controller/README.md:10:1. Each declared pre-start relay failure reaches degraded without delaying usable TCP only after safe DNS is confirmed, or reaches failed when any mandatory base capability is absent. 2. Runtime relay, framing, process, or relay-lane failure atomically stops new UDP admission, invalidates all association IDs, cancels pumps, closes queues and timers, and publishes the new mode for one generation. 3. Existing and new TCP flows follow the existing M1 transport policy while degraded and no UDP datagram is rerouted to a physical or unrelated local socket. 4. Duplicate failures, simultaneous safe-DNS loss, provider stop, late relay callbacks, and replacement generation events produce one ordered cleanup and no transient false-full snapshot. 5. Fault-injection tests cover every reason, failure at every bootstrap and runtime boundary, cleanup counters, TCP preservation, safe-DNS gate, stop races, and bounded transition latency.
EPIC-260715-2lz67t_udp-relay-and-degraded-mode/STORY-260715-2ungml_capability-and-degraded-mode/TASK-260715-uh8kk6_enforce-degraded-udp-rejection-and-no-fallback-policy/README.md:4:Apply explicit traffic policy while degraded: preserve eligible TCP, keep DNS on the selected safe tunnel transport, reject general UDP at the private HEV adapter boundary, expose the later QUIC policy seam, and prove no packet is opened on a physical interface.
EPIC-260715-2lz67t_udp-relay-and-degraded-mode/STORY-260715-2ungml_capability-and-degraded-mode/TASK-260715-uh8kk6_enforce-degraded-udp-rejection-and-no-fallback-policy/README.md:7:In scope: mode-gated UDP association admission; deterministic HEV or application-visible fast failure supported by the stack; close existing associations; DNS classification before general rejection; TCP capability unchanged when healthy; physical-socket factory prohibition; counters by broad reason; transition ordering; QUIC-family policy hook without final M3 behavior; provider stop and failed mode; unit and route-sentinel tests. Out of scope: synthesizing arbitrary ICMP if the stack contract does not support it, blocking system exceptions beyond Apple guarantees, final QUIC heuristics, fail-closed includeAllNetworks, path reconnect, per-application rules, analytics, and destination logging.
EPIC-260715-2lz67t_udp-relay-and-degraded-mode/STORY-260715-2ungml_capability-and-degraded-mode/TASK-260715-uh8kk6_enforce-degraded-udp-rejection-and-no-fallback-policy/README.md:10:1. In degraded mode tunnel DNS is admitted only to the safe DNS component, eligible TCP continues through M1, and every new general UDP association receives the documented bounded rejection without relay or physical socket creation. 2. Transition from full stops new UDP admission before capability publication and existing associations are closed or failed deterministically with no datagram accepted after invalidation. 3. Failed and stopping modes admit neither TCP, DNS, nor UDP through stale runtime generations and cannot report degraded merely because the system VPN session remains connected. 4. Factory and sentinel tests prove no degraded general UDP or ordinary DNS opens a physical-interface socket or reaches a physical resolver, including timeout and fallback branches. 5. Metrics expose only aggregate admitted, rejected, closed, DNS, and reason counts and test snapshots contain no destinations, queries, payloads, or per-app identifiers.
EPIC-260715-2lz67t_udp-relay-and-degraded-mode/STORY-260715-3pv7qc_relay-portable-build-assets/TASK-260715-1ue4oy_generate-and-embed-relay-asset-manifest/README.md:7:In scope: manifest schema and version; Linux and macOS x86_64 or arm64 normalization; stable ordering; asset file name; protocol version; exact byte size; lowercase SHA-256; build identity; build provenance reference; generator; application-bundle inclusion; typed read-only client lookup; duplicate, missing, extra, stale, and mismatched asset validation. Out of scope: remote uname parsing, upload, application signing, runtime manifest updates, network fetch, protocol handshake implementation, and final release attestation.
EPIC-260715-2lz67t_udp-relay-and-degraded-mode/STORY-260715-3pv7qc_relay-portable-build-assets/TASK-260715-mocqmr_add-portable-asset-ci-smoke-and-boundary-tests/README.md:7:In scope: four target jobs; native execution where available and documented emulation otherwise; binary format and architecture inspection; unprivileged user; read-only working directory where feasible; version, self-hash, and stdio handshake smoke; stdout contamination sentinel; stderr redaction; unsupported arguments; EOF and signal exit; listener or child-process checks; manifest tampering; artifact and log retention. Out of scope: remote SSH upload, live destination UDP, physical iPhone traffic, App Store signing, public network scans, and treating emulation as the only release evidence for a target with required native runners.
EPIC-260715-2mqgvm_viability-and-foundation/README.md:10:Gate A0 is deferred off this epic's critical path under ADR-013 and stays required before App Store distribution and iOS submission, where it must have documented evidence; Gate P0 installs a packet-tunnel provider on the physical Apple-silicon Mac; the physical-iPhone row is a named deferred gap under ADR-024, never inferred from Mac results and never a pass; packet bridge/HEV and one SSH engine pass the M0 matrix; open decisions are recorded with reproducible results.
EPIC-260715-2mqgvm_viability-and-foundation/STORY-260715-2xnj3v_apple-capability-provisioning/README.md:1:# Gate P0 — Apple capabilities and physical-device provisioning
EPIC-260715-2mqgvm_viability-and-foundation/STORY-260715-2xnj3v_apple-capability-provisioning/README.md:4:Prove that the Relux Works organization can provision the required host and packet-tunnel extension identities and can install and launch a minimally functional custom VPN provider on the physical Apple-silicon Mac. iOS is deferred under ADR-024; its identifiers stay defined so the iPhone row re-arms unchanged. The result is a reproducible signing and entitlement baseline for later M0 integration, not a production VPN.
EPIC-260715-2mqgvm_viability-and-foundation/STORY-260715-2xnj3v_apple-capability-provisioning/README.md:7:In scope: organization enrollment and required roles; bundle ID, App Group, and Keychain access-group naming; Network Extension packet-tunnel capabilities; development certificates and profiles; disposable iOS and macOS host/provider probes; NETunnelProviderManager configuration; entitlement and embedded-profile inspection; physical-device install, launch, start, and stop evidence. Out of scope: TestFlight or App Store release, Developer ID notarization, production UI, packet forwarding, SSH transport, relay behavior, and storage of certificates, private keys, credentials, or provisioning secrets in the repository or board.
EPIC-260715-2mqgvm_viability-and-foundation/STORY-260715-2xnj3v_apple-capability-provisioning/README.md:10:1. Evidence confirms Relux Works organization enrollment, team identifier, responsible roles, registered physical devices, and access to manage identifiers, capabilities, certificates, and profiles. 2. A reviewed identity matrix defines unique iOS and macOS host and extension bundle IDs plus the minimum App Group and Keychain groups. 3. Host and extension App IDs and development profiles contain the exact required packet-tunnel, App Group, and Keychain entitlements with no unrelated capability. 4. A disposable provider is installed through its containing app, saves a NETunnelProviderManager configuration, launches, reports a versioned probe result, and stops cleanly on one named Apple-silicon Mac; the physical-iPhone row is a named deferred gap under ADR-024. 5. Exported entitlement and profile inspections match the planned matrix and contain no secret material. 6. A Gate P0 record includes device and OS versions, Xcode and source revisions, bundle/profile identifiers, commands or steps, observed results, failures, and an explicit pass or fail verdict.
EPIC-260715-2mqgvm_viability-and-foundation/STORY-260715-2xnj3v_apple-capability-provisioning/TASK-260715-1jckn0_build-disposable-ios-packet-tunnel-probe/README.md:10:1. The iOS host embeds exactly one packet-tunnel extension and both targets sign with the approved development identities and entitlements. 2. The host can create or update only its own NETunnelProviderManager configuration using the extension bundle identifier. 3. Provider launch returns a versioned probe payload through app messaging and stop cancels all work without packet forwarding. 4. Clean build and install instructions record Xcode, SDK, source revision, signing inputs by non-secret identifier, and expected outputs. 5. Automated or scripted archive inspection fails on entitlement drift, mismatched application identifier, or a missing embedded extension.
EPIC-260715-2mqgvm_viability-and-foundation/STORY-260715-jnpbyz_packet-bridge-hev-spike/BUG-260720-2zh86a_hev-xcframework-deployment-target-mismatch-blocks-apple-matrix/README.md:4:PRE-EXISTING (surfaced by TASK-260720-2sltje validation): the full make validate-native Apple build matrix stops before Xcode builds because the HEV XCFramework declares a macOS 10.14/11.0 minimum that mismatches the project deployment targets (macOS 15.0 / iOS 18.0 per ADR-016/3r0993). Task-specific static inspection + validate-core pass, but the full Apple-matrix build is blocked. Must be fixed before the packet-plane Apple builds and physical validation (12x6oq etc.) and before CI full matrix. Likely in the 1g9cyt native seam / build-apple hook or the HEV XCFramework Info.plist minimums. Fix so all native XCFrameworks (HEV, ReluxLibSSH2) declare minimums compatible with iOS 18/macOS 15, and validate-native builds the full matrix.
EPIC-260715-2mqgvm_viability-and-foundation/STORY-260715-jnpbyz_packet-bridge-hev-spike/README.md:4:Prove on the physical Apple-silicon Mac — the physical-iPhone row is deferred with iOS under ADR-024 as a named gap, never inferred from Mac results and never a pass — that public Network Extension packetFlow APIs can exchange bounded datagrams with unmodified upstream HEV and bundled lwIP through a nonblocking AF_UNIX SOCK_DGRAM socket pair, with correct Darwin family framing, backpressure, cleanup, instrumentation, MTU behavior, and an extension memory baseline.
EPIC-260715-2mqgvm_viability-and-foundation/STORY-260715-jnpbyz_packet-bridge-hev-spike/README.md:7:In scope: pinned upstream HEV, core, task-system, and lwIP revisions and notices; Swift PacketFlowBridge; Darwin four-byte family headers; nonblocking socket buffers and effective-limit inspection; bounded batching and time budgets; reason-specific drops and fatal-error semantics; fixed HEV UDP-in-TCP mode; process-local SOCKS boundary; IPv4 and IPv6 traffic; MTU 1500, 4096, and 8500; socket pressure; repeated lifecycle and concurrency tests; physical footprint and available-memory evidence; fork-versus-upstream decision. Out of scope: private utun discovery, file-descriptor scanning, SSH transport, remote UDP relay, production route or DNS policy, lane scheduling, full application UX, and performance claims not backed by before-and-after measurements.
EPIC-260715-2mqgvm_viability-and-foundation/STORY-260715-jnpbyz_packet-bridge-hev-spike/README.md:10:1. The spike uses only NEPacketTunnelFlow and documented Darwin socket APIs and contains no utun control access, descriptor discovery, or private API. 2. Forward and reverse paths preserve packet boundaries, add or validate and strip network-byte-order AF_INET or AF_INET6 headers, batch writes, and count malformed or unsupported frames. 3. EAGAIN, EWOULDBLOCK, and ENOBUFS cause bounded reason-specific drops; EMSGSIZE fails start with attempted and effective limits; persistent errors and cancellation release all tasks and descriptors. 4. Upstream HEV runs with socks5.udp set to tcp and the stated low-memory baseline, and its internal SOCKS endpoint rejects unowned ingress. 5. Swift Testing and fault-injection coverage verifies framing, batching budgets, saturation, MTU errors, cleanup, repeated start and stop, IPv4, and IPv6. 6. Physical evidence on the physical Apple-silicon Mac covers MTUs 1500, 4096, and 8500; the physical-iPhone row is a named deferred gap under ADR-024, never inferred from Mac results and never a pass; constrained buffers; receiver stalls; nominal mixed traffic; staged concurrency; and lifecycle loops with device, OS, revisions, traffic shape, counters, descriptors, CPU, syscall rate, physical footprint, peak, and available-memory samples. 7. A gate report selects measured MTU, buffer, batch, and session baselines, states whether the 25–30 MiB target is met, and permits a HEV fork only when Instruments evidence shows a material bottleneck and a measured callback improvement.
EPIC-260715-2mqgvm_viability-and-foundation/STORY-260715-jnpbyz_packet-bridge-hev-spike/TASK-260715-2jatnd_record-m0-packet-bridge-decision/README.md:4:Consolidate implementation, deterministic tests, fuzzing, physical MTU, pressure, lifecycle, and memory evidence into the M0 Bridge gate decision, including initial configuration and the measured fork-versus-upstream disposition.
EPIC-260715-2mqgvm_viability-and-foundation/STORY-260715-jnpbyz_packet-bridge-hev-spike/TASK-260715-2jatnd_record-m0-packet-bridge-decision/README.md:7:In scope: public-API compliance; correctness; selected MTU, socket buffers, batch budgets, and session baseline; error and drop behavior; physical-device memory headroom; known gaps; upstream pin; notice status; fork decision; revalidation triggers; downstream M1 readiness. Out of scope: SSH or relay gate claims, production route and DNS approval, future performance guarantees, and authorizing a fork without Instruments evidence.
EPIC-260715-2mqgvm_viability-and-foundation/STORY-260715-jnpbyz_packet-bridge-hev-spike/TASK-260715-2jatnd_record-m0-packet-bridge-decision/README.md:10:1. A TASK-ID-scoped ADR links every required M0 Bridge row to reproducible evidence and marks pass, fail, or blocked without averaging away a red platform or address-family result. 2. The record selects initial MTU, requested socket buffers, batch count and time budgets, HEV settings, and measured session ceiling with rationale. 3. Pass requires public API only, correct IPv4 and IPv6 framing, bounded backpressure, clean lifecycle, no nominal unexplained loss, and physical-device memory headroom. 4. A fork is rejected by default and allowed only when Instruments identifies bridge copies or syscalls as a material bottleneck and a measured callback prototype improves it with regression coverage and rebase plan. 5. Residual risks, device and OS revalidation triggers, notices, and tasks unblocked for M1 are explicit.
EPIC-260715-2mqgvm_viability-and-foundation/STORY-260715-jnpbyz_packet-bridge-hev-spike/TASK-260715-35wctc_add-hev-bridge-integration-tests/README.md:4:Exercise the real unmodified HEV stack through PacketFlowBridge in the macOS harness and Apple-provider test seams, proving IPv4 and IPv6 TCP translation, SOCKS contract behavior, configuration, backpressure propagation, and clean lifecycle before physical performance runs.
EPIC-260715-2mqgvm_viability-and-foundation/STORY-260715-jnpbyz_packet-bridge-hev-spike/TASK-260715-3dn813_add-packet-flow-bridge-unit-and-fault-tests/README.md:7:In scope: IPv4 and IPv6 family words and payload-version agreement; empty, undersized, unknown-family, truncated, zero-length, and oversized datagrams; packet boundaries; count/time budgets; all requested/effective socket buffer gauges; normalized Darwin would-block behavior; ENOBUFS, EMSGSIZE, peer EOF, PacketFlow rejection, persistent errors, first-error races, cancellation at every startup stage, late callbacks, repeated lifecycle, and descriptor/task/registration accounting. Out of scope: real HEV behavior, physical-device throughput, SSH, routes, DNS, private APIs, arbitrary sleeps, and hard-coded final tuning values.
EPIC-260715-2mqgvm_viability-and-foundation/STORY-260715-l2i2oo_target-project-architecture/README.md:10:1. An architecture decision records the chosen generator, exact supported deployment targets, target graph, module ownership, dependency direction, configuration strategy, and reasons against viable alternatives. 2. Clean generation produces buildable ReluxProxyMac, ReluxProxyMacTunnel, ReluxProxyIOS, ReluxProxyIOSTunnel, ReluxTunnelCore, ReluxTunnelHarness, relux-relay, and protocol-test targets with deterministic identifiers and schemes. 3. Both providers are thin adapters that depend on shared core contracts and do not duplicate packet, SSH, relay, lifecycle, or memory state machines. 4. Native Swift and C dependencies can be pinned, licensed, and linked without dynamic-library or App Store extension violations. 5. Credential-free CI or local verification builds every target and runs shared tests; signed physical-device configurations consume Gate P0 identities without committing secrets. 6. The existing SwiftPM tests, manual SOCKS app build, defaults, and release path remain reproducible until an explicit migration decision says otherwise. 7. Developer documentation explains generation, target ownership, signing inputs, artifact locations, and how to run the harness.
EPIC-260715-2mqgvm_viability-and-foundation/STORY-260715-l2i2oo_target-project-architecture/TASK-260715-33oofa_add-ios-host-and-packet-tunnel-targets/README.md:10:1. Generated ReluxProxyIOS embeds exactly one ReluxProxyIOSTunnel with identifiers and entitlements derived from the approved matrix. 2. The provider imports NetworkExtension, exposes only minimal lifecycle and version-message entry points, and contains no live packet or transport implementation. 3. Host and provider build for the selected simulator and device SDK configurations without manual Xcode edits; physical signing consumes Gate P0 inputs. 4. Automated target tests verify bundle relationships, version compatibility, Info.plist values, and entitlement-file contents. 5. No iOS target depends on the legacy macOS executable sources.
EPIC-260715-2mqgvm_viability-and-foundation/STORY-260715-l2i2oo_target-project-architecture/TASK-260715-3r0993_select-project-generator-and-deployment-target-policy/README.md:7:In scope: current supported Tuist and viable alternatives; deterministic generation and pinning; Xcode and Swift toolchain support; Network Extension, Network.framework, Swift concurrency, selected dependency requirements, physical-device coverage, CI runner availability, and upgrade policy. Out of scope: installing tools globally, generating the project, lowering targets through private APIs or compatibility shims, and choosing SSH or relay implementations.
EPIC-260715-2mqgvm_viability-and-foundation/STORY-260715-l2i2oo_target-project-architecture/TASK-260715-3r0993_select-project-generator-and-deployment-target-policy/README.md:10:1. A dated TASK-ID-scoped research artifact compares the supported generator choices against deterministic output, extension targets, signing configuration, dependency integration, CI maintenance, and Relux Works conventions. 2. The chosen tool and version or version range, pinning mechanism, bootstrap command, and upgrade owner are explicit. 3. Exact minimum iOS and macOS versions are selected from current API, dependency, device, Xcode, and CI evidence rather than preference. 4. The policy names the current physical iPhone and Apple-silicon Mac baseline, records the iPhone baseline as deferred with iOS under ADR-024 while keeping it defined so it re-arms unchanged, plus the oldest versions that must build and test. 5. Any unsupported assumption becomes a blocking research item instead of a speculative configuration.
EPIC-260715-2mqgvm_viability-and-foundation/STORY-260715-l2i2oo_target-project-architecture/TASK-260715-nphtib_execute-generated-project-architecture-verification/README.md:7:In scope: clean-checkout generation; project and scheme enumeration; iOS simulator and macOS builds; signing-disabled device build where supported; Swift Testing; native archive inspection; harness cancellation; relay target matrix; legacy SwiftPM and packaging regression; deterministic second generation. Out of scope: fixing failures, physical Gate P0 device testing, packet or SSH matrices, release signing, and feature acceptance.
EPIC-260715-2mqgvm_viability-and-foundation/STORY-260715-l2i2oo_target-project-architecture/TASK-260715-pmww4f_add-relux-tunnel-harness-target/README.md:10:1. ReluxTunnelHarness builds via SPM as a standalone macOS executable (NO generated Xcode/Tuist workspace required — that workspace is gated behind Gate P0 only; Gate A0 is deferred off this path under ADR-013 and remains required before App Store distribution) and links the same ReluxTunnelCore products intended for the providers. 2. A no-op smoke command records source revision, dependency revisions, configuration, duration, platform, and metric schema in deterministic machine-readable output. 3. Signal cancellation and normal exit release temporary files, sockets, and tasks with tested exit codes. 4. Subcommands can inject clocks, transports, packet endpoints, pressure signals, and fault policies without importing app UI. 5. Swift Testing covers argument validation, result-schema versioning, cancellation, and cleanup.
EPIC-260715-2mqgvm_viability-and-foundation/STORY-260715-l2i2oo_target-project-architecture/TASK-260715-sbrrp7_add-credential-free-project-build-validation/README.md:7:In scope: pinned-tool bootstrap; deterministic generation drift checks; macOS host and provider Debug/Release builds with signing disabled; explicit verification that deferred iOS targets and schemes are absent from the macOS-only graph; ReluxTunnelCore and harness tests; relay target-shell and native-packaging checks; static entitlement and embedding inspection; legacy SwiftPM regression; local clean-checkout entry point; equivalent macOS pull-request workflow definition; privacy-safe artifact and log locations. Out of scope: generating or building deferred iOS targets, production certificates, physical Gate P0 repetition, Linux CI, TestFlight, notarization, DMG publication, full SBOM policy, packet feature tests, and SSH host conformance already owned by their tasks.
EPIC-260715-2mqgvm_viability-and-foundation/STORY-260715-l2i2oo_target-project-architecture/TASK-260715-sbrrp7_add-credential-free-project-build-validation/README.md:10:1. One documented local entry point and equivalent macOS pull-request job definition run deterministic generation, every active macOS credential-free build, shared Swift Testing suites, relay smoke, native linkage checks, and legacy regression checks. 2. The gate fails on generator drift, missing or unexpected target/scheme, invalid embedding, entitlement-file mismatch, unpinned dependency input, disallowed linkage, accidental deferred-iOS activation, or legacy build regression. 3. Logs and artifacts identify source revision, tool versions, SDKs, deployment targets, and invoked schemes without exposing secrets. 4. Signing-required and deferred-iOS checks are explicitly reported as NOT RUN with reasons, never as passed. 5. The local validation completes from a clean checkout using documented prerequisites, and the checked-in PR workflow invokes the same entry point without duplicating policy.
EPIC-260715-2mqgvm_viability-and-foundation/STORY-260715-l2i2oo_target-project-architecture/TASK-260819-2lu7p6_validate-generated-macos-target-development-signing/README.md:7:In scope: regenerate/install the host macOS Development profile with System Extension capability, restore an authenticated Xcode account session, permit the Apple Development private key for codesign, build the checked-in generated target definitions without weakening entitlements, inspect the embedded signed provider, and preserve privacy-safe evidence. Out of scope: changing product entitlements to fit a stale profile, Developer ID distribution, notarization, runtime forwarding, or iOS.
EPIC-260715-2mqgvm_viability-and-foundation/STORY-260715-lkshfz_ssh-engine-spike/README.md:10:1. Both candidates build in the macOS and iOS packet-tunnel target configurations, with pinned revisions, licenses, integration constraints, and reproducible build commands recorded. 2. One common conformance harness obtains raw host-key evidence before acceptance and exercises Ed25519 plus an approved fallback against current OpenSSH, documented older profiles, and the real relux host. 3. Candidate evidence covers independent direct-tcpip and bidirectional long-lived exec channels, early close and half-close, bounded backpressure, cancellation, network loss, reconnect, and no task, channel, socket, or descriptor growth across repeated cycles. 4. Initial receive windows and WINDOW_ADJUST behavior are configurable per channel; tests cover 32 KiB, 64 KiB, and capped bandwidth-delay-product profiles without multiplying a large default by maximum sessions. 5. Client byte and time thresholds and server requests survive at least 5 GiB of simultaneous direct-tcpip and exec traffic, with lane-local failure evidence. 6. Hundreds of concurrent channels and one, two, and four connection trials remain within the extension memory budget and record device, OS, revisions, algorithms, traffic, loss and latency, windows, queued bytes, CPU, physical footprint, peak, and failures. 7. A decision record selects only a candidate that passes every required gate, documents rejected results and residual risks, and routes a red primary candidate to the alternate rather than weakening criteria.
