EPIC-260715-2qzczm_resilience-and-performance/STORY-260715-2indo6_ssh-lane-pool-and-scheduler/README.md:10:1. One mandatory control lane starts first and optional general or bulk lanes open only after identity, server-limit, and memory admission checks. 2. Every lane proves the same approved host identity and credential generation, and any mismatch closes that lane before channels open. 3. New flows are assigned from documented congestion and policy inputs, remain pinned until close, and Lane D never retroactively adopts an existing flow. 4. Control, relay, and latency-sensitive DNS remain serviceable under general or bulk congestion within recorded latency bounds. 5. Rekey and failed-lane states stop new assignment on the affected lane, preserve valid existing channel state where possible, and route control-lane loss through an explicit degraded or recovery transition without DNS or UDP leakage. 6. Queues, lanes, and channels stay inside configured ceilings and soft memory pressure suppresses optional capacity. 7. Deterministic tests and physical Apple-silicon Mac trials, with iPhone rows deferred-unavailable under ADR-024, at one, two, and four lanes record loss, latency, throughput, queues, windows, channels, memory, CPU, errors, and cleanup and demonstrate measured head-of-line improvement without resource growth.
