EPIC-260715-2qzczm_resilience-and-performance/STORY-260715-3ao1u9_nat64-sleep-and-captive-validation/README.md:10:1. A versioned device, OS, interface, address-family, route-mode, event, traffic, and capture matrix names every required row, fixture, metric, expected outcome, and evidence location before physical execution. 2. IPv6-only and NAT64 starts and transitions use the actual connected endpoint and an exact current-generation exclusion, preserve IPv4 and IPv6 application behavior where supported, and never deadlock in stale routes. 3. Sleep and wake produce at most one current reconnect sequence, cancel stale work, restore only truthful capability, and keep reconnect overlap within the memory ceiling. 4. Captive-network handling matches compatible and fail-closed disclosures and Apple exceptions while ordinary traffic and DNS remain tunneled or fail explicitly. 5. Containing-app termination does not tear down a healthy provider, while cancellation, provider stop, and repeated lifecycle loops release routes, tasks, timers, channels, associations, sockets, and descriptors. 6. Deterministic fault tests cover synthesized-address changes, sleep races, captive transitions, UI termination, stop during start, late callbacks, and API capability changes. 7. Physical Apple-silicon Mac evidence records every supported row, keeps iPhone rows as explicit deferred-unavailable entries under ADR-024, identifies red or unavailable rows without waiver, and publishes regression triggers when OS behavior differs.
