EPIC-260715-3810we_tcp-dns-system-vpn/STORY-260715-2wjwuf_ssh-profile-auth-and-host-verification/TASK-260715-3t2v9w_implement-profile-driven-ssh-session-bootstrap/README.md:10:1. No default tunnel route is installed by this service and endpoint resolution plus connection complete on the pre-tunnel physical path. 2. Host policy accepts before credential retrieval or authentication, and all failure orderings are observable in tests. 3. Successful bootstrap returns one selected-engine session bound to the canonical profile, verified host identity, credential generation, and actual connected IPv4 or IPv6 endpoint. 4. Ed25519 and the approved fallback key type authenticate against supported fixtures, while rejection, timeout, cancellation, and host change close sockets and session state. 5. The service exposes only privacy-safe algorithm, timing, endpoint-family, and error metrics and never retains secret material after authentication setup.
EPIC-260715-3810we_tcp-dns-system-vpn/STORY-260715-2wjwuf_ssh-profile-auth-and-host-verification/TASK-260728-7ii1xz_decide-macos-sysex-credential-transport/README.md:7:In scope: the exact question the spec leaves open — by what mechanism does a root-context macOS NEPacketTunnelProvider obtain SSH private-key material and an optional passphrase that the user-context containing app holds, without placing secrets in providerConfiguration, App Group data, logs, or board resources; comparison of at least host-to-provider IPC seeding over NETunnelProviderSession/XPC, the file-based System keychain with an access group, and provider-owned root-context storage; on-demand and boot-time start behaviour when the containing app is not running; secret lifetime and zeroization implications; the resulting amendment (grant or keep prohibited) for keychain-access-groups on works.relux.tunnel.mac.tunnel in the TASK-260715-ypo7yo matrix; the entitlement and portal consequences of the selected option; a physical reachability check on this Apple-silicon Mac where one is needed to settle a documented uncertainty. Out of scope: implementing the vault or the resolver, iOS behaviour, host trust policy, key import or generation, engine selection, UI, and changing the identifier matrix directly — this task produces the amendment, TASK-260715-ypo7yo owns the matrix.
EPIC-260715-3810we_tcp-dns-system-vpn/STORY-260715-2wjwuf_ssh-profile-auth-and-host-verification/TASK-260728-7ii1xz_decide-macos-sysex-credential-transport/README.md:10:1. The artifact states the exact open question verbatim and cites the two Apple-sourced facts that create it: a macOS NE system extension runs as root outside a user context, and the Data Protection Keychain plus keychain access groups only share items between programs running as the same user. 2. At least three candidate transports are compared on unattended/on-demand start with the containing app not running, secret lifetime and zeroization, blast radius if the provider is compromised, entitlement and portal cost, and testability; each candidate states what it CANNOT guarantee. 3. One transport is selected with rationale, or the task records a concrete blocker with options, tradeoffs, and a recommendation rather than a guessed design. 4. The selection produces an explicit amendment verdict for keychain-access-groups on works.relux.tunnel.mac.tunnel in the TASK-260715-ypo7yo matrix: either grant with the exact literal, or keep prohibited with the reason; the verdict names TASK-260715-ypo7yo as the applying owner. 5. Where documentation alone cannot settle reachability, a privacy-safe physical check on this Apple-silicon Mac records the observed result with commands and exit codes and no secret value; where no check was possible the limitation is named, never assumed away. 6. The affected downstream tasks TASK-260715-379cpk, TASK-260715-1o9wjz, TASK-260715-29ws8l, TASK-260715-2hhh7x, and TASK-260715-3f4lxy are listed with the specific assumption each must revise.
